WHAT WE WANT FROM YOU

A stock of candidates in the file stock/candidates.jsonl: one JSON object per line, carrying
the identity fields above, a "found_by" field saying which query or strategy found it, and
two fields that are the point of your work:

  "evidence"  what you actually read that shows this candidate fits the definition, quoted:
              the sentence of the project's own documentation, the lines of the diff, the
              signature and the comment, whatever the definition is about. Copied from the
              source, not summarised.
  "judgment"  one or two sentences IN YOUR OWN WORDS saying why this candidate is what the
              definition describes, naming the part of the definition it satisfies and how
              the evidence shows it.

A CANDIDATE IS SOMETHING YOU HAVE LOOKED AT. A file whose name matches a pattern, a commit a
query returned, a repository with the right stars: those are SUSPECTS. A suspect becomes a
candidate when you have opened it and can say why it fits. A pattern that stands in for the
definition is not a judgment: "the file contains int main" is not "a runnable command-line
program", "the commit modified a Java file" is not "a method whose body changed while its
comment did not". Whoever builds this instance later starts from your line and pays minutes
per candidate; a line you did not look at costs them that and is worse than no line at all.

Finding candidates is cheap on purpose: no builds, no timings, no test runs. Construction
happens later, on this stock, and a candidate that turns out not to work THERE is a normal
loss, not your failure. What is your failure is a candidate that does not exist, that plainly
fails a rule above, or that the definition plainly excludes and you would have seen it by
looking.

How many: {{ instance.sample_size }} is what we would like. Fewer candidates you have judged
are worth more than the target reached with lines nobody read; stop when the budget runs out
and say what you have.

HOW YOU WORK

You decide where to search and how. Plan first, then work in two passes.

  Pass 1, in bulk, with scripts: find suspects and gather what is needed to judge them.
  Query a code host, walk clones, save the results to files. For each suspect fetch the
  material the definition is about and store it beside the suspect: the README and the build
  file, the commit's diff, the method's text before and after, the analyzer's output. Scripts
  do this a hundred at a time and cost nothing against the API quota when they work from a
  clone. Cheap mechanical filters belong here, to throw away what is obviously not a
  candidate before you read anything.

  Pass 2, by reading: go through the material yourself, in batches, and decide. Print what
  you gathered for twenty suspects at a time and read it; keep the ones that fit, with the
  evidence and your judgment; throw away the rest. This is the only place a candidate is
  made. Where the material is long, script the extraction, never the decision. Read the
  whole of what you judge: when a rule or the definition is about a commit message, read the
  whole message (`git show -s --format=%B <commit>`), not its subject line -- a squash merge
  often names its details only in the body.

Record the candidates you accept with stock_add: give it one candidate (a JSON object), a list
of them, or JSON lines, and it appends them to stock/candidates.jsonl, skips any already there,
and tells you how many lines the file holds. It never removes a line. A candidate leaves the
stock only through stock_remove (see WHEN YOU ARE DONE). Never write the stock file any other
way -- not with write_to_file, str_replace, a script, `>`, `mv` or `rm`: a rewrite replaces the
whole file, and when it goes wrong every candidate already in it is gone. The file grows over
the run; keep it valid at every step (one complete JSON object per line, nothing else).

RECORD A CANDIDATE THE MOMENT YOU ACCEPT IT. A candidate not written to stock/candidates.jsonl
is lost: your prompt replays only your last twenty actions, with older outputs cut short, and
the count at the top of every prompt counts only that file. A candidate you judged good and
kept in your head, in a scratch file or in a script's output is gone within a few cycles, and
you will search for it again. Do not wait for a batch, for the check script, or for more
candidates: when one passes your reading, add it with stock_add now, then go on searching.

KEEP EACH REPLY SMALL. A reply that is cut off at the output limit runs nothing, and a reply
that carries a whole program is the one that gets cut off. It is good practice to split long
logic over several scripts, one per reply: write and run the first one in this reply, the next
one in the next reply, and so on, each reading what the previous one saved to a file. A long
script goes into its own script file (a .py or .sh file) in parts: write_to_file for the first
part, then str_replace to add the next; a long literal list (keywords, names, paths) goes into a
file of its own, or is kept short. Candidates never go through write_to_file: they go to the
stock with stock_add and leave it with stock_remove.

Your machine is a Linux container with git, curl, python3 and pip, and network access. Your
working directory is /workspace, and every path in these instructions is relative to it:
the stock is /workspace/stock/candidates.jsonl, the known-set /workspace/known/known.jsonl.
Commands run there with linux_terminal (pass a timeout for long ones) or in the background
with launch_background and poll_process; give poll_process a wait in seconds and it waits
that long for the job before reporting, so a long job costs you one turn, not one per
minute. Files are written with write_to_file and
str_replace and read with read_file, by their relative path. Python packages you need
(requests, for one) are installed with pip.

The GitHub token is in the environment as GITHUB_TOKEN and in /root/.tokens.env. The host's
API quota is small and SHARED with the other agents searching in parallel: 5,000 calls an
hour and 30 searches a minute in total, so calls per repository exhaust it in minutes. Use
the API only to enumerate repositories: one search page returns a hundred of them with
their stars, language, fork flag, push date and issue setting already in it. Everything
about a repository's contents and history comes from a CLONE and scripts over it, which
costs nothing against the quota: the files in its tree, its README and documentation, its
commit count and its commits with their messages and diffs, its merged pull requests as
recorded in its history. A bare clone with --filter=blob:none carries the full history
cheaply; delete clones when you are done with them. `git ls-remote` confirms that a
repository and a commit exist without the API.
