known/known.jsonl lists candidates already found: by earlier runs, and by the other agents
searching in parallel with you, as far as they have recorded them. The system refreshes it
before every step, so it grows while you work; look at it again before you read a repository
in depth. Its candidates do not count towards your target; skip them, and skip their
repositories where the spread rule says one per repository.

WHEN YOU ARE DONE

Write stock/check.sh: a script that walks stock/candidates.jsonl and confirms, for every
line, what a script CAN confirm: that the candidate exists exactly as recorded (the
repository, the commit, the file or the pull request the identity names, whichever apply);
that its repository satisfies each admissibility rule listed above, observed the way the rule
says; that the mechanical part of the definition holds where there is one (the commit touches
the file the line names, the method is in it, the two sides differ); and that every line
carries a non-empty "evidence" and "judgment". For every line that fails, it prints the
line's identity and what failed; it exits 0 only when every line passes. Facts you read off a
search page can have changed or been misread, so the check re-observes them.

THE CHECK ONLY READS. check.sh never writes, edits or rewrites stock/candidates.jsonl or any
other file in stock/: it reports, and you decide. A check that rewrites the stock turns one
bug in the check into a lost stock -- a script with a shell typo that "fails" every line and
then writes back the lines that passed writes back nothing.

WHEN THE CHECK FAILS, FIND OUT WHY BEFORE YOU REMOVE ANYTHING. The check is code you just
wrote, and it is as likely to be wrong as a candidate. In this order:

  1. Read the check's whole output and its error messages. When many lines fail at once, or
     all of them fail with the same message, suspect the script first: a shell error ("too
     many arguments", "command not found", "No such file"), a wrong path, a clone or fetch
     that never ran, a field read under the wrong name. Fix the script, not the stock, and run
     it again.
  2. Take each line that still fails and check it by hand: run, for that one candidate, the
     command the check runs, and read what it returns; open the commit, the file, the
     repository. A candidate you verified while searching that now "fails" points at the
     check, not at the candidate.
  3. Only a candidate you have confirmed by hand fails the definition or a rule leaves the
     stock: remove it with stock_remove, naming it and saying what you checked and what it
     showed. If you cannot tell whether the candidate fails or the check is wrong, keep the
     candidate and fix the check.

A REMOVAL NEEDS THE EVIDENCE AN ADDITION NEEDS. Whenever you take a candidate out -- after a
check, or because you changed your mind about it -- read the whole source again (the whole
commit message, the whole diff, the file) and quote in the stock_remove reason the words or
lines that show it fails. If you accepted the candidate earlier, look at the evidence you
recorded for it then: when that evidence is still in the source, the candidate stays. When in
doubt, keep the line: a doubtful candidate costs the builder a few minutes; a valid one
removed is lost.

THIS HOLDS FOR ANY SCRIPT OR COMMAND OF YOURS, not only check.sh. Whenever any script or
command -- the check, a "verify a sample" one-off, a re-run of your miner -- says that many
candidates fail at once, or all with the same message, suspect the script first. Before you
believe it, prove one of those candidates by hand the other way: fetch the commit
(`git fetch --depth 1 <repository url> <commit>` into an empty `git init` directory) and read
it. `git ls-remote` lists only branch and tag tips: it cannot see an older commit, so a
"not found" from it says nothing about whether a commit exists.

If a confirmed failure makes you doubt the method that produced a whole batch, change the
method for the NEXT candidates and re-check the ones you hold one by one; keep every line
that passes.

The check is the cheap half. The definition itself was judged by YOU, per candidate, in pass
2: a script cannot read a README or tell a real inconsistency from a formatting change, and a
check written to pass whatever the stock happens to hold proves nothing. Do not turn the
judgment into a pattern here.

A CHECK THAT RE-RUNS THE FILTER THAT PRODUCED THE CANDIDATES VERIFIES NOTHING. If your search
kept every .c file containing `int main` and your check confirms that a .c file of that name
exists, every line passes and you have learned nothing: the check agreed with the filter, and
both were wrong about the definition. Write the check against the DEFINITION and the clauses
above, observed independently of how you found the candidate, and expect it to flag some of
your own lines. A check that flags nothing on a stock you assembled in bulk is a check that
is not looking.
It runs in a fresh container from the same image that holds only your stock/ directory,
copied to /stock and used as the working directory, and the token; read candidates.jsonl by
that relative name and rely on nothing else from your machine (packages you pip-installed
are not there either; the script's shebang chooses its interpreter). To try it on your own
machine the same way, run it from inside stock/: `cd stock && sh check.sh`. Do not prove existence
through the API: its shared quota is usually exhausted when a check runs, and a quota error
then reads as "not found". `git ls-remote <repository url>` proves a repository (and a
branch or tag; it lists only branch and tag tips, so it cannot prove an older commit);
`git fetch --depth 1 <repository url> <commit>` into an empty `git init`
directory proves a commit; a file at a commit is proved from that fetched commit with
`git cat-file -e <commit>:<path>`. Then call stock_verify: the system runs your check.sh, counts
the distinct new candidates, and ends the run when the script exits 0 and the count reaches
{{ instance.sample_size }}. Otherwise it tells you what is short and you continue. The run also ends
when the budget is spent; what is in the stock then is kept.

